PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE

REGIONAL REGULATION OF PARIGIMOUTONG REGENCY

Number 1 Year 2018

CONCERNING

PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE

BY THE GRACE OF GOD ALMIGHTY

REGENT OF PARIGIMOUTONG

Considering:

a. that every citizen has the right to be free from torture or treatment that degrades human dignity and has the right to obtain security and freedom from all forms of Violence;
b. that all forms of Violence against Women and Children constitute violations of human rights so that their dignity must be protected and their right to life guaranteed in accordance with their nature and destiny without discrimination;
c. that Violence against Women and Children continues to increase, so that protection efforts are required;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b, and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning the Protection of Women and Children Victims of Violence;

In View of:

1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 7 Year 1984 concerning Ratification of the Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1984 Number 29, State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3877);
3. Law Number 39 Year 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
4. Law Number 10 Year 2002 concerning the Establishment of Parigi Moutong Regency in Central Sulawesi Province (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2002 Number 23, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4185)
5. Law Number 23 Year 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235) as amended by Law Number 35 Year 2014 (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2014 Number 297, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5606);
6. Law Number 23 Year 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
7. Law Number 13 Year 2006 concerning Protection of Witnesses and Victims (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2006 Number 64, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4635) as amended by Law Number 31 Year 2014 (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2014 Number 293, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5602);
8. Law Number 21 Year 2007 concerning the Eradication of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2007 Number 58: Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);
9. Law Number 23 Year 2014 concerning Regional Government [State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587) as last amended by Law Number 9 Year 2015 (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2015 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5679);
10. Government Regulation Number 9 Year 2008 concerning Procedures and Mechanisms for Integrated Services for Witnesses and/or Victims of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2008 Number 22, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4818);
11. Presidential Decree Number 36 Year 1990 concerning Ratification of the Convention on the Rights of the Child (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1990 Number 57);
12. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 2 Year 2008 concerning Guidelines for the Implementation of Women's Protection;
13. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 3 Year 2008 concerning Guidelines for the Implementation of Child Protection;
14. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 1 Year 2010 concerning Minimum Service Standards in the Field of Integrated Services for Women and Children Victims of Violence;
15. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 5 Year 2010 concerning Guidelines for the Establishment and Development of Integrated Service Centers;
16. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 2 Year 2011 concerning Guidelines for Handling Child Victims of Violence;
17. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 19 Year 2011 concerning Guidelines for the Empowerment of Women Victims of Violence;

With Mutual Approval

REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF PARIGI MOUTONG REGENCY

and

REGENT OF PARIGIMOUTONG

HAS DECIDED:

To Establish:

REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING THE PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE.

Article 2

Women and child victims of violence receive rights, including:
a. the right to be respected for their dignity as human beings;
b. the right to recovery;
c. the right to determine their own decisions;
d. the right to obtain information;
e. the right to confidentiality;
f. the right to social rehabilitation;
g. the right to complaint handling that is fast, appropriate, comfortable and according to needs;
h. the right of Victims and their families to obtain facilities in the judicial process;
1. the right to Assistance;
J. the right to a sense of security; and
k. the right to restitution.

Article 3

Child victims of violence in addition to receiving rights as referred to in Article 4, also receive special rights, including:
a. the right to survival, growth and development;
b. the right to basic population services;
c. the right to equal protection;
d. the right to be free from various stigma; and
e. the right to obtain freedom.

Article 4

The Regional Government is responsible for efforts to protect women from acts of violence that occur in public or private life in the form of:
a. physical violence;
b. sexual violence;
c. economic violence;
d. social violence;
e. psychological violence;
f. domestic neglect;
g. coercion or deprivation of liberty; and
h. threats of certain actions.

Article 5

Every person who hears, sees or knows of the occurrence of Violence as referred to in Article 4 shall make efforts to:
a. prevent the continuation of criminal acts;
b. provide protection to victims;
c. provide emergency assistance; and
d. assist in the complaint process and submission of applications for protection orders.

Article 6

1. The Regional Government shall prevent acts of violence against women by:
a. socializing laws and regulations;
b. empowering and raising awareness among families, parents and the community by providing information, guidance and/or counseling;
c. making efforts to increase the quantity and quality of formal, non-formal and informal education;
d. making efforts to build community participation and concern to implement prevention and protection of women and children from violence in an integrated community-based manner;
e. building networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, universities, religious institutions/organizations and various non-governmental organizations in efforts to prevent violence against women and children;
f. establishing a violence prevention system, mapping locations or areas prone to violence;
g. increasing community awareness that respects women and protects children;
h. establishing UPTD PPA in every Sub-district, and Women and Children Protection Service Posts at the Village and Sub-district levels; and
i. establishing a women and children protection task force.
2. In addition to the Regional Government, prevention as referred to in paragraph (1) must be implemented by:a. family and/or closest relatives;
b. community;
c. vertical agencies;
d. law enforcement apparatus:e. educational institutions;
f. health institutions;
g. religious institutions/organizations;
h. community social institutions;
1. business sector; and
j. other institutions.
3. Prevention of acts of violence against women as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out in an integrated manner and coordinated by the Regional Apparatus having the main duties and functions in the field of women's empowerment and child protection.

Article 8

1. The Regional Government shall implement integrated services for victims of acts of violence, through activities:
a. complaint services;
b. health services;
c. counseling;
d. psychological services;
e. spiritual guidance;
f. social rehabilitation services;
g. legal aid and assistance services;
h. safe house services; andy ori. repatriation and social reintegration.
2. In the event that the Regional Government does not yet have counseling personnel as referred to in paragraph (1) letter c, it may request assistance from social workers, volunteer assistants, and/or spiritual counselors.
3. Services as referred to in paragraph (1) must be supported by facilities in the form of:
a. special service rooms within the police force;
b. expert and professional personnel;
c. service centers and safe houses; and /orod. other facilities and infrastructure necessary for the recovery of victims including perpetrators.
4. The provision of services to victims of acts of violence shall be carried out in an integrated manner by the Regional Government together with other social institutions.
5. Other social institutions as referred to in paragraph (4) may receive and send case referrals from other service units in a networked manner.
6. Integrated services for victims of acts of violence as referred to in paragraphs (1) through (5) shall be implemented in accordance with standard operating procedures.
7. Provisions regarding standard operating procedures as referred to in paragraph (6) shall be regulated in a Regent Regulation.

Article 10

1. Health services as referred to in Article 8 paragraph - (1) letter b, shall be implemented using health facilities owned by the Regional Government or other referral hospitals by providing medical treatment and health recovery services to victims.
2. Health services as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be performed by health personnel to victims in accordance with professional standards, standard operating procedures and the medical needs of victims.
3. Health services as referred to in paragraph (1), through efforts to:
a. take anamnesis from victims;
b. examine victims;
c. provide first aid;
d. restore physical and psychological health, make efforts to prevent disease and other health disorders;
e. provide counseling;
f. refer to more adequate health service facilities if necessary, may refer to other parties with a multidisciplinary approach, including health services, medicolegal, psychosocial assistance and legal aid; and/org. provide information to the police.
4. In addition to the efforts as referred to in paragraph (3) for certain cases, health personnel may provide reproductive health services in accordance with medical needs.
5. In providing services as referred to in paragraphs (3) and (4), health personnel must create medical records in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations,
6. For every medical action to be performed by health personnel, there must be informed consent for medical action from the victim or their family in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
7. For investigation purposes, authorized health personnel must prepare a visum et repertum and/or visum et repertum psichiatricum. or prepare a medical certificate.
8. Health personnel have the right to receive legal protection.

Article 11

Legal aid and protection services as referred to in Article 8 paragraph (1) letter g include:
a. providing protection in a safe house;
b. providing legal information to victims;
c. providing assistance for victims as witnesses from the investigation process to the verdict;
d. providing special legal protection for child victims of acts of violence may be carried out by appointing guardianship in accordance with laws and regulations.

Article 16

1. Prevention of acts of violence against children in addition to being implemented by the Regional Apparatus as referred to in Article 14 paragraph (2), also involves the parties:
a. parents, family and / or closest relatives;
b. community;
c. vertical agencies;
d. law enforcement apparatus;
e. educational institutions;
f. health institutions;
g. religious institutions/organizations;h. social community institutions;
1. child social welfare institutions;
J. business sector; and
k. other institutions engaged in the field of child protection care.
2. Provisions regarding the involvement of parties as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be regulated in a Regent Regulation.

Article 18

Rehabilitation services as referred to in Article 14 letter c shall be carried out in the form of:
a. legal aid services;
b. health rehabilitation services;
c. social rehabilitation services;
d. repatriation and social reintegration services; and/ or
e. restitution services for child victims of violence.
2. In rehabilitation services as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government may cooperate with:
a. vertical agencies;
b. provincial government;
c. other regency/city governments;
d. village/sub-district government;
e. non-governmental organizations;
f. private parties;
g. community; and/or
h. family.

Part Two

Special Protection of Children Article 19 (1) Special protection of children shall be provided to:
a. children in emergency situations;
b. children in conflict with the law;
c. children from minority and isolated groups;
d. children who are exploited economically and/ or sexually;
e. children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic, and other addictive substance abuse;
f. children who are victims of pornography;
g. children with HIV/ AIDS;
h. child victims of kidnapping, sale, and /or trafficking;
1. child victims of physical and / or psychological Violence;
J. child victims of sexual crimes;
k. child victims of terrorism networks;
1. children with disabilities:m. child victims of maltreatment and neglect;
n. children with deviant social behavior; and
o. children who are victims of stigmatization from labeling related to their parents' condition.
2. The Regional Government in implementing the handling of special protection of children as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 21

1. P2TP2A as referred to in Article 20 shall have the duties to:
a. provide services;
b. provide protection to victims of acts of violence, reporters and witnesses; and
c. conduct empowerment of victims of acts of violence.
2. Services as referred to in paragraph (1) letter a shall include:
a. receiving complaints/reports of Violence against women and/ or children, consultation, and counseling;
b. receiving and sending case referrals from other Regency/City Regional Governments or institutions established by other Regency/City Regional Governments;
c. providing legal assistance;
d. health;
e. social rehabilitation;
f. legal aid services; and
g. repatriation and social reintegration.

Article 22

In handling child victims of acts of violence as referred to in Article 18, the Regional Government may cooperate with:
a. vertical agencies;
b. provincial government;
c. other regency /city governments;
d. village /sub-district government;
e. non-governmental organizations;
f. private parties;
g. community; and/orh. family.

Article 23

1. In certain cases the Regional Government may cooperate with;
a. other Regional Governments;
b. police;
c. advocates;
d. health workers;
e. psychologists:f. social workers;
g. volunteer companions;
h. spiritual counselors;
i. other law enforcers, to assist Victims in the court proceedings;
J. National Commission on Violence Against Women;
k. Indonesian Child Protection Commission or Child Protection Institution; and/or
l. certain parties desired for the benefit of the victim.
2. Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) may be in the form of:
a. exchange of data and information;
b. socialization;
c. provision of legal aid:d. health services;
e. psychological psychiatric services;
f. victim assistance;
g. counseling;
h. social rehabilitation;
i. spiritual guidance; and/or
j. repatriation and social reintegration.

Article 24

1. Obligations and responsibilities in providing protection to (1) women and children from acts of violence shall be the joint responsibility of the Regional Government and the Community.
2. Obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in paragraph (1). shall include:
a. establishing, implementing policies, programs, and conducting cooperation activities in the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence;
b. facilitating the establishment of institutional protection of women and children from acts of violence and providing support for facilities and infrastructure;
c. allocating budget for the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence according to the financial capacity of the Region;
d. fostering and supervising the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence;
e. providing protection services for women and child victims of acts of violence;
f. encouraging and increasing community participation; and
g. appointing parents and/ or substitute family caregivers as a protection measure for children who are victims of acts of violence.

CHAPTER VII

COMMUNITY AND BUSINESS SECTOR PARTICIPATION

Article 25

1. Every person may participate in providing protection to women and children from acts of violence.
2. Participation as referred to in paragraph (1) may be carried out by:
a. preventing and stopping the occurrence of acts of violence;
b. providing protection to victims;
c. providing first aid to victims of acts of violence;
d. reporting acts of violence against women and /or children;
e. independently socializing the rights of women and children;
f. strengthening social community institutions in handling acts of violence against women and children; and/ or
g. disseminating information about laws and regulations related to acts of violence against women and children.
